        "biography": "Enzo Barboni (1922-2002) was an Italian film director and cinematographer well known for his western films. Born on January 7, 1922, in Rome, Italy, Barboni initially worked as a camera operator before making his directorial debut in the 1960s. He gained international recognition for multiple collaborations with Terence Hill and Bud Spencer, directing popular films such as \"They Call Me Trinity\" (1970) and \"Trinity Is Still My Name\" (1971), which became iconic in the Spaghetti Western canon. Barboni's films were known for their blend of action, comedy, and charismatic performances.",
